# AI on the phone

## Overview

This notebook runs machine-learning models on the phone's CPU, with no GPU
or cloud service: object detection on a camera photo, speech to text, and a
chat with a small language model. First it looks at the CPU they run on and
at how slowly plain Elixir tensors run on it. It builds on the earlier
notebooks: Wi-Fi (01), the camera (05) and audio (10).

## The stack

The AI stack has three kinds of parts:

* **Capability packages** do the work, each a native library wrapped for
  the BEAM: [`llama_cpp_ex`](https://hexdocs.pm/llama_cpp_ex) runs language
  models (llama.cpp), [`whisper_cpp`](https://hexdocs.pm/whisper_cpp)
  transcribes speech (whisper.cpp, with Silero voice detection) and
  [`evision`](https://hexdocs.pm/evision) runs the YOLO object detector
  (OpenCV).
* **`nerves_ai`** is the composer. It puts one small facade in front of each
  capability (`NervesAI.LLM`, `NervesAI.STT`, `NervesAI.Vision`), downloads
  model files with checksums (`NervesAI.Models`), reads and writes WAV files
  (`NervesAI.Audio.WAV`) and runs every call in a "burst" that holds the CPU
  at full speed.
* **`nerves_ai_fp3`** is the profile that describes this phone. At boot it
  pins the VM's threads that run native code to the fast cluster and
  tells the engines to use four threads. `cpu_governor` provides the
  CPU topology, frequency control and thread pinning; `nerves_data_resize`
  grows the data partition on first boot so models fit.

Nothing is configured to start at boot. This notebook downloads each model
when you need it and starts the matching capability with `Kino.start_child/1`.

## The CPU

The Snapdragon 632 has two clusters of four Kryo 250 cores: `cpu0-3` are
the slower "Silver" cores and `cpu4-7` the faster "Gold" cores. Neither
cluster has the dot-product or half-precision instructions that newer ARM
chips use to speed up model maths, so the native libraries are built for
plain ARMv8.0. `cpu_governor` reads the layout from `/sys`:

```elixir
CpuGovernor.topology()
```

What can this user change on this phone? Frequency control, temperature
readings and thread pinning:

```elixir
CpuGovernor.capabilities()
```

### Where the VM's threads run

The `nerves_ai_fp3` profile pinned the BEAM's *dirty* schedulers (where
native code such as llama.cpp runs, and whose threads the engines' worker
pools inherit their CPUs from) to the Gold cores `cpu4-7`, and the normal
schedulers to `cpu0-3`. `CpuGovernor.Affinity.report/1` lists the threads.
`outside` holds the dirty threads that are allowed on a CPU outside the
expected ones:

```elixir
gold = CpuGovernor.fastest_cpus()
report = CpuGovernor.Affinity.report(expect: gold)

for t <- report.by_class.dirty do
  %{thread: t.name, allowed_cpus: Enum.join(t.allowed, ","), last_ran_on: t.cpu}
end
|> Kino.DataTable.new()
```

```elixir
%{expected: gold, outside: length(report.outside)}
```

### Does the performance burst help?

`CpuGovernor.with_performance/1` holds the Gold cluster at full speed for
the duration of a function and restores the previous setting afterwards.
The `nerves_ai` facades do this around every model call.

The work here is a SHA-256 over 64 MB: the crypto NIF runs large inputs on a
dirty scheduler, which the profile pinned to the Gold cores.

Two things would skew a naive comparison, so the cell avoids them: the first
runs after idle are slow while the clock ramps up (two warm-up rounds), and
acquiring a lease has a cost of its own (a few milliseconds once the layout
is cached; the first call also detects the topology). The table therefore
times the work *inside* the lease, alternates the two variants, and reports
the lease cost separately. All values are milliseconds.

```elixir
data = :crypto.strong_rand_bytes(64_000_000)

time_ms = fn fun ->
  {us, result} = :timer.tc(fun)
  {Float.round(us / 1000, 1), result}
end

work = fn -> :crypto.hash(:sha256, data) end

# warm-up: ramp the clock and populate the lease server's layout cache
for _ <- 1..2 do
  work.()
  CpuGovernor.with_performance(fn -> :ok end)
end

{lease_ms, _} = time_ms.(fn -> CpuGovernor.with_performance(fn -> :ok end) end)

rounds =
  for _ <- 1..5 do
    {plain, _} = time_ms.(work)
    {inside, _} = CpuGovernor.with_performance(fn -> time_ms.(work) end)
    {plain, inside}
  end

median = fn values -> values |> Enum.sort() |> Enum.at(div(length(values), 2)) end

%{
  lease_overhead_ms: lease_ms,
  plain_ms: Enum.map(rounds, &elem(&1, 0)),
  inside_lease_ms: Enum.map(rounds, &elem(&1, 1)),
  median_plain: median.(Enum.map(rounds, &elem(&1, 0))),
  median_inside_lease: median.(Enum.map(rounds, &elem(&1, 1)))
}
```

If the two rows match, the cores were already at full speed during the plain
runs (the schedutil governor ramps within tens of milliseconds, so bursts
shorter than that gain little) or `/sys` is read-only (see `capabilities/0`
above). A lease pays off for bursts of a few hundred milliseconds and longer,
such as model inference.

## Tensors on the BEAM

[Nx](https://hexdocs.pm/nx) is Elixir's tensor library. Its default
`Nx.BinaryBackend` is written in plain Elixir: portable, and very slow on a
phone. This firmware sets [`NxEigen.Backend`](https://hexdocs.pm/nx_eigen) as
the default (`config :nx, default_backend: NxEigen.Backend`): the Eigen C++
library with NEON, built for this CPU. Here is what a 640×640 RGB `u8` image
costs on each backend for the steps a YOLO pre-processing needs, plus a
512×512 matrix product (milliseconds, best of three). Your notebook process
runs on a normal scheduler, so on the Silver cores.

```elixir
best_ms = fn fun ->
  1..3
  |> Enum.map(fn _ ->
    {us, _} = :timer.tc(fn -> fun.() end)
    us / 1000
  end)
  |> Enum.min()
  |> Float.round(1)
end

bench = fn backend ->
  img = Nx.broadcast(Nx.u8(128), {640, 640, 3}) |> Nx.backend_copy(backend)
  floats = Nx.as_type(img, :f32)
  a = Nx.iota({128, 128}, type: :f32, backend: backend)

  %{
    pad: best_ms.(fn -> Nx.pad(img, 114, [{80, 80, 0}, {0, 0, 0}, {0, 0, 0}]) end),
    transpose: best_ms.(fn -> Nx.transpose(img, axes: [2, 0, 1]) end),
    as_type_f32: best_ms.(fn -> Nx.as_type(img, :f32) end),
    divide_255: best_ms.(fn -> Nx.divide(floats, 255) end),
    dot_128: best_ms.(fn -> Nx.dot(a, a) end)
  }
end

%{
  eigen: bench.(NxEigen.Backend),
  binary: bench.(Nx.BinaryBackend)
}
```

The object detector below does its image work in OpenCV, which has its own
NEON code; Nx is there for your own tensor code and for libraries such as
`nx_image` and `nx_signal`. (The binary backend's `dot_128` is already slow;
a 512×512 product takes it about a minute on a fast desktop.)

## Models: downloaded when you need them

The next steps use trained models. They're too big for the firmware and
none is downloaded automatically: each step fetches its model the first
time with `NervesAI.Models.ensure_one/3`. It checks the SHA-256, writes the
file atomically to `/data/models` and skips the download when the file is
already there.

The phone needs internet access for the first run. If this doesn't say
`:internet`, connect to Wi-Fi with the Wi-Fi notebook:

```elixir
VintageNet.get(["connection"])
```

The Fairphone 3 profile has a catalog of models known to run on this phone,
with their checksums:

```elixir
catalog = NervesAIFP3.Profile.catalog()
Map.keys(catalog)
```

Two helpers for the steps below: `fetch` downloads a model (printing
progress) and returns its path, and `start` starts a capability under this
notebook's supervisor and waits for its model to load.

```elixir
fetch = fn id, spec ->
  progress = fn bytes, total ->
    IO.puts("#{id}: #{div(bytes, 1_000_000)} of #{(total && div(total, 1_000_000)) || "?"} MB")
  end

  case NervesAI.Models.ensure_one(id, spec, on_progress: progress, progress_step: 50_000_000) do
    {:ok, path} ->
      path

    {:error, reason} ->
      raise "Couldn't download #{id}: #{inspect(reason)}. " <>
              "Is the phone online? Check with the Wi-Fi notebook."
  end
end

start = fn adapter, config ->
  case Kino.start_child(adapter.child_spec(config)) do
    {:ok, _pid} -> :ok
    {:error, {:already_started, _pid}} -> :ok
    {:error, reason} -> raise "Couldn't start #{inspect(adapter)}: #{inspect(reason)}"
  end

  ready =
    Enum.any?(1..240, fn _ ->
      adapter.ready?(config) or (Process.sleep(500) && false)
    end)

  if ready, do: :ready, else: raise("#{inspect(adapter)} didn't load its model; see the log")
end
```

## Find objects in a photo (YOLO)

**YOLOv5n** finds and labels objects in a picture: people, chairs,
bottles, laptops and 76 other everyday categories (the COCO dataset;
`NervesAI.Vision.COCO.labels/0` lists them). The model is 4 MB.
`NervesAI.Vision.Evision` runs it with OpenCV and `NervesAI.Vision` is the
interface to call.

```elixir
yolo_path = fetch.(:yolov5n, catalog.yolov5n)

start.(NervesAI.Vision.Evision,
  adapter: NervesAI.Vision.Evision,
  path: yolo_path
)
```

### Take a photo and detect

Point the back of the phone at something and run the cell. It takes a
2×2-binned photo (quicker than full resolution) and lists what the model
found. Each box is `[x1, y1, x2, y2]` in pixels of the photo.

```elixir
photo = "/tmp/detect.jpg"
IO.puts("Taking the photo...")
{:ok, _} = Fp3Camera.snap(:rear, photo, binned: true)
IO.puts("Looking for objects...")

detections = NervesAI.Vision.detect(NervesAI.Vision, photo)

for d <- detections do
  %{object: d.label, confidence: Float.round(d.score, 2), box: inspect(Enum.map(d.box, &round/1))}
end
|> Kino.DataTable.new()
```

`detect/3` takes `conf:` (minimum score, default 0.25) and `iou:` options.
`NervesAI.Vision.annotate/4` writes a copy of the photo with the boxes
drawn on it; this cell draws them as an SVG instead.

### See it

```elixir
{height, width, _channels} = Evision.imread(photo).shape

boxes =
  for %{box: [x1, y1, x2, y2], label: label, score: score} <- detections do
    """
    <rect x="#{x1}" y="#{y1}" width="#{x2 - x1}" height="#{y2 - y1}"
          fill="none" stroke="yellow" stroke-width="#{width / 250}"/>
    <text x="#{x1 + 6}" y="#{y1 + width / 30}" fill="yellow" font-size="#{width / 32}"
          font-family="sans-serif">#{label} #{round(score * 100)}%</text>
    """
  end

jpeg = Base.encode64(File.read!(photo))

Kino.HTML.new("""
<svg viewBox="0 0 #{width} #{height}" style="max-width: 100%">
  <image href="data:image/jpeg;base64,#{jpeg}" width="#{width}" height="#{height}"/>
  #{Enum.join(boxes)}
</svg>
""")
```

## Speech to text (Whisper)

**Whisper** turns speech into text. This is OpenAI's `tiny.en` model
(English only, about 75 MB) in the format whisper.cpp reads, run by
`NervesAI.STT.WhisperCpp`. A second, tiny model (about 0.9 MB), **Silero**,
detects where speech is so silence is skipped before Whisper sees it.

```elixir
whisper_path =
  fetch.(:whisper,
    source: {:hf, "ggerganov/whisper.cpp", "ggml-tiny.en.bin"},
    path: "/data/models/ggml-tiny.en.bin",
    sha256: "921e4cf8686fdd993dcd081a5da5b6c365bfde1162e72b08d75ac75289920b1f"
  )

vad_path =
  fetch.(:silero,
    source: {:hf, "ggml-org/whisper-vad", "ggml-silero-v5.1.2.bin"},
    path: "/data/models/ggml-silero-v5.1.2.bin",
    sha256: "29940d98d42b91fbd05ce489f3ecf7c72f0a42f027e4875919a28fb4c04ea2cf"
  )

start.(NervesAI.STT.WhisperCpp,
  adapter: NervesAI.STT.WhisperCpp,
  path: whisper_path,
  vad_path: vad_path,
  language: "en"
)
```

### Transcribe a recording

The firmware ships an 11-second excerpt of John F. Kennedy's 1961
inaugural address. Nothing here converts sample rates, so the audio has to
be 16 kHz; this file is.

```elixir
sample = Application.app_dir(:nerves_livebook_fp3, "priv/audio/jfk.wav")
IO.puts("Transcribing...")
{:ok, %{text: text, segments: segments}} = NervesAI.STT.transcribe(NervesAI.STT, {:wav, sample})

IO.puts(text)
Kino.DataTable.new(segments)
```

### Transcribe yourself

Records 5 seconds from the phone's main microphone. Speak (in English)
while it runs. The microphone needs a system image whose kernel drives the
WCD9335 audio codec; without it, `arecord` fails here.

The recording is asked for at 16 kHz mono, and ALSA's `plughw` device
converts from the microphone's rate. **This has not been tested on the
device.** If `arecord` rejects `-r 16000`, record at 48 kHz as the audio
notebook (10) does; `NervesAI.STT.transcribe/3` then answers
`{:error, {:unsupported_rate, 48000}}` and the recording needs a resampling
step first.

```elixir
microphone = [
  {"MultiMedia2 Mixer SLIMBUS_0_TX", :on},
  {"SLIM TX7 MUX", "DEC7"},
  {"ADC MUX7", "DMIC"},
  {"DMIC MUX7", "DMIC1"},
  {"AIF1_CAP Mixer SLIM TX7", :on}
]

for {name, value} <- microphone, do: {name, ExAudio.cset(name, value)}

arecord = fn seconds, path ->
  System.cmd("arecord", ~w(-D plughw:0,1 -f S16_LE -r 16000 -c 1 -d #{seconds} #{path}),
    stderr_to_stdout: true
  )
end

# The first capture after boot returns silence; a short one wakes the path up.
arecord.(1, "/tmp/warmup.wav")
IO.puts("Recording for 5 seconds: speak now!")
{output, status} = arecord.(5, "/tmp/me.wav")
if status != 0, do: IO.puts(output)

IO.puts("Transcribing...")
NervesAI.STT.transcribe(NervesAI.STT, {:wav, "/tmp/me.wav"})
```

## Chat with a language model (TinyLlama)

**TinyLlama 1.1B Chat** is a small language model: about 670 MB, the
biggest step. `NervesAI.LLM.LlamaCpp` runs it with llama.cpp. The model
file carries its own chat template, so you pass the conversation as a list
of messages and the library formats the prompt the way TinyLlama was
trained.

```elixir
llm_path = fetch.(:tinyllama, catalog.tinyllama)

start.(NervesAI.LLM.LlamaCpp,
  adapter: NervesAI.LLM.LlamaCpp,
  path: llm_path,
  n_ctx: 1024
)
```

### Ask a question

Change `question` and run the cell again. The answer is printed piece by
piece as the model writes it. `max_tokens` caps its length.

```elixir
question = "What three things should I see in Brussels?"

messages = [
  %{role: "system", content: "You are a helpful, concise travel guide."},
  %{role: "user", content: question}
]

started = System.monotonic_time(:millisecond)

pieces =
  NervesAI.LLM.stream_chat(NervesAI.LLM, messages, max_tokens: 128, temp: 0.7)
  |> Enum.reduce(0, fn
    text, count when is_binary(text) ->
      IO.write(text)
      count + 1

    {:error, reason}, count ->
      IO.puts("\n[generation failed: #{inspect(reason)}]")
      count
  end)

seconds = (System.monotonic_time(:millisecond) - started) / 1000
IO.puts("\n\n#{pieces} pieces in #{Float.round(seconds, 1)} s")
```

`NervesAI.LLM.chat/3` takes the same arguments and returns the whole answer
at once.

## Sound, generated

The audio notebook (10) plays tones. `NervesAI.Audio.WAV` writes and reads
the WAV files: samples are plain floats between -1.0 and 1.0.

```elixir
rate = 48_000
volume = 0.1
notes = [262, 294, 330, 349, 392, 440, 494, 523]

melody =
  for freq <- notes, n <- 0..(div(rate, 4) - 1) do
    volume * :math.sin(2 * :math.pi() * freq * n / rate)
  end

:ok = NervesAI.Audio.WAV.write("/tmp/melody.wav", melody, rate: rate)
System.cmd("aplay", ["-D", "plughw:0,0", "/tmp/melody.wav"], stderr_to_stdout: true)
```

### How loud was it?

`NervesAI.Audio.WAV.read/1` returns the samples of any WAV file as mono
floats. The recording from the speech step is split into tenths of a
second, and each bar shows the loudest sample in that slice.

```elixir
{:ok, %{rate: rate, pcm_f32: pcm}} = NervesAI.Audio.WAV.read("/tmp/me.wav")

for(<<s::float-32-little <- pcm>>, do: abs(s))
|> Enum.chunk_every(div(rate, 10))
|> Enum.map(fn slice ->
  peak = Enum.max(slice)
  String.duplicate("█", round(peak * 50)) <> " #{Float.round(peak, 2)}"
end)
|> Enum.join("\n")
|> IO.puts()
```

